### Parse JSON request body in Route Handler

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.2.2/docs/01-app/02-guides/backend-for-frontend.mdx

Use request.json() to parse JSON payloads in POST handlers. Available in both TypeScript and JavaScript.

```typescript
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const res = await request.json()
  return Response.json({ res })
}
```

```javascript
export async function POST(request) {
  const res = await request.json()
  return Response.json({ res })
}
```

--------------------------------

### Handle webhooks in Route Handler

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.2.2/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/route.mdx

Process incoming webhook payloads from third-party services using a POST Route Handler. No additional bodyParser configuration is required unlike Pages Router API Routes.

```typescript
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    const text = await request.text()
    // Process the webhook payload
  } catch (error) {
    return new Response(`Webhook error: ${error.message}`, {
      status: 400,
    })
  }

  return new Response('Success!', {
    status: 200,
  })
}
```

```javascript
export async function POST(request) {
  try {
    const text = await request.text()
    // Process the webhook payload
  } catch (error) {
    return new Response(`Webhook error: ${error.message}`, {
      status: 400,
    })
  }

  return new Response('Success!', {
    status: 200,
  })
}
```

--------------------------------

### HTTP Methods /route.js

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/v16.2.2/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/route.mdx

Route Handlers support standard HTTP methods and allow for custom request logic using Web Request and Response APIs.

```APIDOC
## GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS /route.js

### Description
Route Handlers allow you to create custom request handlers for a given route using the Web Request and Response APIs.

### Method
GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS

### Endpoint
/route.js

### Parameters
#### Request Body
- **request** (NextRequest) - Optional - An extension of the Web Request API providing access to cookies and nextUrl.

### Request Example
export async function GET(request) {
  return Response.json({ message: 'Hello World' })
}

### Response
#### Success Response (200)
- **message** (string) - A sample response message.

#### Response Example
{
  "message": "Hello World"
}
```

--------------------------------

### App Router route returning non-2xx JSON error response

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/examples/with-ably/app/api/send-message/route.ts

Route handler that checks for a missing env var and returns a 500 JSON error response with NextResponse.json and an explicit error status

```typescript
import * as Ably from "ably";
import { type NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";
import type { ProxyMessage, TextMessage } from "../../../types";

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  if (!process.env.ABLY_API_KEY) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: "Missing ABLY_API_KEY environment variable" },
      { status: 500 },
    );
  }

  const body = (await request.json()) as ProxyMessage;
  const client = new Ably.Rest(process.env.ABLY_API_KEY);
  const channel = client.channels.get("some-channel-name");

  const message: TextMessage = {
    text: `Server sent a message on behalf of ${body.sender}`,
  };
  await channel.publish("test-message", message);

  return NextResponse.json({ ok: true });
}
```

--------------------------------

### App Router route handlers receive Web NextRequest and return Web Response

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/src/server/route-modules/app-route/module.ts

App Router route handlers receive a standard Web API NextRequest (extends Request) and must return a Web Response. They do NOT receive Node IncomingMessage/ServerResponse.

